---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CasterWidget from '../components/CasterWidget.tsx';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Quick Cast Your Screen — QuiiCast"
	description="Quick cast your screen in seconds — a free screen share app that works on desktop and Android. Start sharing and get a 4-digit code, no account needed."
	path="/caster"
>
	<div class="text-center">
		<h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white sm:text-4xl">Quick cast your screen</h1>
		<p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-md text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
			Click start, allow screen sharing, and give the 4-digit code to whoever you want to share
			your screen with — up to 5 people can join with the same code. It expires after a few minutes.
		</p>
	</div>
	<div class="mx-auto mt-10 max-w-lg">
		<CasterWidget client:load />
	</div>

	<section class="prose mx-auto mt-20 max-w-2xl">
		<h2>How casting your screen works</h2>
		<p>
			QuiiCast turns your browser tab into a screen-sharing sender. Click <strong>Start sharing my
			screen</strong> above, and your browser will ask which screen, window, or tab to share — pick
			whichever keeps anything private out of view. Once you confirm, QuiiCast generates a random
			4-digit code and shows you a live preview of exactly what your viewers will see.
		</p>
		<p>
			Send that code to anyone you want watching — over text, chat, email, or read out loud on a
			call. They enter it on the <a href="/receiver">receive page</a> and connect directly to your
			browser over a peer-to-peer WebRTC connection; the video never passes through QuiiCast's own
			servers. Up to 5 people can join the same code at once, and the code automatically refreshes
			if it sits unused long enough to expire, so a long session doesn't get interrupted.
		</p>

		<h2>Getting a better connection</h2>
		<p>
			If everyone watching is on the same WiFi network as you — the same office, the same house —
			turn on <strong>Prefer local WiFi</strong> before you start sharing. It keeps the actual video
			traffic on your local network instead of routing it over the internet, which usually means
			lower latency and a smoother picture. Leave it off (the default) when anyone watching is on a
			different network, since it can't find a path between two networks on its own.
		</p>
		<p>
			The <strong>Quality</strong> selector controls resolution and frame rate. Auto adapts to your
			connection automatically; for anything with fast motion (games, video), a higher frame-rate
			preset matters more than resolution. For mostly-static content like a document or terminal, a
			lower preset looks just as sharp and is easier on a slow connection.
		</p>

		<h2>FAQ</h2>
		<p>
			<strong>Does the viewer need an account or an app?</strong><br />
			No — they just need a browser and the 4-digit code. There's nothing to install on either side.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Can I share just one window instead of my whole screen?</strong><br />
			Yes — when your browser's share picker appears, choose a specific window or tab rather than
			"Entire Screen" to keep everything else on your desktop private.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Why does it say screen sharing isn't supported?</strong><br />
			This usually means the browser or device doesn't expose screen capture to websites — notably
			iOS Safari and other iPhone browsers, which Apple restricts at the OS level. Android Chrome and
			any desktop browser support it.
		</p>
		<p>
			Read more on the <a href="/how-it-works">how it works</a> page, or check the full
			<a href="/faq">FAQ</a> for questions about receiving a share instead of sending one.
		</p>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
